---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";

const linkedinProjectUrl =
	"https://www.linkedin.com/in/gustavo-sakamoto-de-toledo/overlay/Project/946097480/treasury/?profileId=ACoAADvQK74BeOIC7J6Hi8fm9KAUlYkVc5nFXHk";

const threatCategories = [
	"Spoofing",
	"Tampering",
	"Repudiation",
	"Information Disclosure",
	"Denial of Service",
	"Elevation of Privilege",
];

const concepts = [
	"Password Managers",
	"Master Password Risk",
	"STRIDE Threat Modeling",
	"Three-Factor Authentication",
	"Decentralized Authentication",
	"Credential Decryption",
	"Vault Access Control",
	"Synthetic Model-Based Evaluation",
];
---

<Layout
	title="Password Manager Authentication Decentralization | Gustavo Sakamoto de Toledo"
	description="Cybersecurity research paper by Gustavo Sakamoto de Toledo proposing a decentralized 3FA framework for password manager authentication."
>
	<header class="subpage-hero">
		<Navbar />

		<section class="section-container project-hero">
			<p class="eyebrow">Cybersecurity Research Paper</p>
			<h1>Password Manager Authentication Decentralization</h1>
			<p class="hero-summary">
				A research paper arguing that traditional password managers concentrate
				security risk around a single master password, then proposing a
				decentralized 3FA framework that separates vault access from credential
				decryption.
			</p>

			<div class="hero-actions">
				<a
					class="primary-button"
					href={linkedinProjectUrl}
					target="_blank"
					rel="noreferrer"
				>
					View on LinkedIn
				</a>
				<a class="secondary-button" href="/#projects">Back to Projects</a>
			</div>
		</section>
	</header>

	<main class="project-page-main">
		<section class="section-container project-overview-grid">
			<div class="project-overview-card">
				<span>Project Type</span>
				<strong>Cybersecurity Research Paper</strong>
			</div>

			<div class="project-overview-card">
				<span>Focus</span>
				<strong>Password Managers, Authentication, and STRIDE</strong>
			</div>

			<div class="project-overview-card">
				<span>Method</span>
				<strong>Synthetic Model-Based Evaluation</strong>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section-container project-case-section">
			<div class="case-section-heading">
				<p class="section-label">Research Problem</p>
				<h2>The master password as a single point of failure</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="case-section-copy">
				<p>
					Traditional password managers often consolidate vault security around
					one master password. While this design improves convenience, it also
					creates a critical point of failure: if the master password or related
					authentication path is compromised, the attacker may gain access to a
					large set of stored credentials.
				</p>

				<p>
					This paper argues that the centralized master-password model can
					amplify risk across the STRIDE threat categories, including spoofing,
					tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service, and
					elevation of privilege.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section-container project-case-section">
			<div class="case-section-heading">
				<p class="section-label">Proposed Design</p>
				<h2>Decentralized 3FA framework</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="case-section-copy">
				<p>
					To reduce the impact of a single compromised authentication factor, the
					paper proposes a decentralized three-factor authentication framework.
					The central idea is to separate vault access from credential decryption,
					so successful access to the password manager does not automatically
					grant the ability to decrypt every stored credential.
				</p>

				<div class="schema-box">
					<code>
						user authentication → vault access<br />
						separate factor validation → credential decryption<br />
						compromise of one layer ≠ full credential exposure
					</code>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section-container project-case-section">
			<div class="case-section-heading">
				<p class="section-label">Threat Model</p>
				<h2>STRIDE categories considered</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="security-list">
				{threatCategories.map((category) => <span>{category}</span>)}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section-container project-case-section">
			<div class="case-section-heading">
				<p class="section-label">Evaluation</p>
				<h2>Synthetic model-based analysis</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="case-section-copy">
				<p>
					The paper uses a synthetic, model-based evaluation to estimate how the
					proposed design could reduce breach risk while maintaining usability.
					Rather than claiming production deployment results, the evaluation
					frames the design as a security architecture improvement and compares
					risk exposure across authentication and decryption boundaries.
				</p>

				<p>
					This approach helped connect architectural design choices to practical
					security outcomes, especially around breach containment, credential
					exposure, and user authentication friction.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section-container project-case-section">
			<div class="case-section-heading">
				<p class="section-label">Concepts</p>
				<h2>Topics covered</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="security-list">
				{concepts.map((concept) => <span>{concept}</span>)}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section-container project-case-section">
			<div class="case-section-heading">
				<p class="section-label">Takeaway</p>
				<h2>Why this matters</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="case-section-copy">
				<p>
					This research connects password manager usability with security
					architecture. It shows how a convenient centralized design can increase
					systemic risk, and how separating authentication from decryption can
					make credential compromise harder for an attacker to fully exploit.
				</p>

				<p>
					The project also demonstrates my ability to use threat modeling,
					security analysis, and technical writing to evaluate authentication
					designs beyond implementation-level code.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
    <Footer />
	</main>
</Layout>